# Auto-send git / MR commands (PowerShell)

After any git-related progress (commit, CI fail, MR blocked, behind main, conflict), **immediately paste copy-pasteable PowerShell commands** in the reply. Do not wait for the user to ask. Do not only describe steps.

## Shell style

- Use `;` not `&&` (Windows PowerShell).
- Prefer exact commands with current branch name filled in.
- One block per action sequence; keep comments to one short Persian line above the block.

## After a successful commit

Always end with push (unless user forbade push):

```powershell
git push -u origin HEAD
```

If the branch already tracks remote:

```powershell
git push
```

## When GitLab says merge conflicts / N commits behind

Do not stop at explanation. Paste this sequence (replace `BRANCH` only if known; otherwise use `HEAD`):

```powershell
git fetch origin main
git merge origin/main
```

If conflicts appear, after resolving files:

```powershell
git add .
git commit -m "merge: sync with origin/main"
git push
```

If user prefers rebase:

```powershell
git fetch origin main
git rebase origin/main
# resolve conflicts, then:
git add .
git rebase --continue
git push --force-with-lease
```

## When CI / build fails after merge to main

1. Identify the TypeScript/build error from the log.
2. Fix on a dedicated branch from up-to-date `main`.
3. Commit, then paste push + remind to open/merge MR.

## Auth / fetch fails in agent environment

If `git fetch`/`push` fails with GitLab username/tty errors, still paste the **exact** commands for the user to run in their own terminal (where they are logged in). Do not pretend the remote was updated.

## Never

- Leave the user with only “merge main then push” in prose.
- Mix unrelated WIP / local scripts into commits.
- Force-push to `main`.
